#!/usr/bin/env bash
# bin/tier-counts — did `bin/prime --db` actually run every tier, and how many?
#
#   ./bin/prime --db 2>&1 | tee /tmp/prime.log
#   ./bin/tier-counts /tmp/prime.log
#
# ## Why this exists at all
#
# `bin/prime` exits zero when it is happy, and `go test` exits zero when a package
# has no test files in it. Those two facts are the same fact: a green exit code is
# a claim, and this repository has three ways to make a false one.
#
#   1. The database tiers do not run at all — someone calls `bin/prime` instead
#      of `bin/prime --db`, and the 44 database tests report as `ok` while the
#      suite never touched a database. darkroom is a service whose entire job is
#      putting bytes in a database.
#   2. They run but touch nothing — `TEST_DATABASE_URL` is unset and every
#      `pgtest.Require` SKIPS with a named reason, and the run is quietly green.
#   3. A suite is gone — a file emptied or renamed, and `go test ./...` prints
#      `ok` for a package with no test files.
#
# None of those change the exit code. All three change the counts, so this reads
# the counts. It is an accounting check, not a second gate: `bin/prime` remains the
# gate, and nothing here loosens it.
#
# # THE IDENTITY, AND WHY IT IS A DIFFERENT ONE FROM THE RUST SCRIPT'S
#
# The Rust version asserted this:
#
#   default `cargo test`      77 passed, 59 ignored
#   `cargo test -- --ignored` 59 passed,  0 ignored
#
# i.e. **the number the default run skips equals the number the database run
# passes.** That identity is available in cargo because a database test is marked
# `#[ignore]` and libtest prints `ignored` as a count.
#
# IT DOES NOT TRANSLATE, and pretending it does would be the exact failure this
# script exists to prevent. A Go test is not marked; it calls `pgtest.Require`,
# which SKIPS at runtime depending on whether a database is reachable. There is no
# build-time marker and no count of tests-that-would-have-been-ignored. Measured
# on this tree, against no database:
#
#   ./internal/darkroomdb/pgtest    3 PASS, 12 SKIP, 15 total
#   ./internal/service             16 PASS, 23 SKIP, 39 total
#
# Sixteen of `service`'s tests and three of `pgtest`'s genuinely need no database, so
# "skipped == passed" is not merely unavailable, it is FALSE here: the skipped
# counts are 35 and the passed counts would be 19.
#
# The `pgtest` figure depends on the ENVIRONMENT and not only on the tree:
# `TestTheLoginRoleCanDoTheWork` reads `DARKROOM_DATABASE_URL` rather than
# `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (`AGENTS.md`), so it PASSES on a machine that has it exported
# and counts 4 rather than 3. That is why the number is a floor and not an equality
# for this package, and why the `--db` gate sets both.
#
# So this script asserts the identity that IS true and IS checkable from one log:
#
#   **the number of tests `bin/prime` ENUMERATES without a database equals the
#   number the database tiers PASS with one.**
#
# Both halves are already in the same log. The `go test -list` step prints
# `    ./internal/service: 39 tests` for every package, and the `--db` step prints
# 39 `--- PASS:` rows for it. The two disagreeing is the failure: a test that
# stopped running in the database tier, a test that stopped being enumerated, a
# package that stopped compiling. And unlike a constant compared against a
# constant, this one keeps holding as tests are added — which is the direction a
# copied-forward constant drifts.
#
# ## The absolute numbers
#
# The floors below are what THIS TREE contained when they were written, read out
# of it with the command in `bin/prime`'s own comment. **Adding a test means
# raising the number in the same commit that adds it.** That is the point: the
# constant is a claim someone has to look at, not a number that drifts.
#
# | step | what is counted | expected |
# |---|---|---|
# | `go test -list` | enumerated per package | ten packages, floors in `bin/prime` |
# | `go test -v ./internal/variants/` | `--- PASS:` rows | 24 |
# | `go test -v ./internal/darkroomdb/...` | `--- PASS:` rows | 17 |
# | `go test -v ./internal/service/...` | `--- PASS:` rows | 39 |
# | `go test -v ./internal/httpapi/` | `--- PASS:` rows | 34 |
#
# The step labels below are the ones `bin/prime` prints. Rewording a step there
# breaks this script, loudly and on the first run, which is the intended coupling:
# the two files must agree about what the tiers are.
#
# # WHAT IS NOT PINNED HERE, AND WHY
#
# The eleven Rust constants — `DB_TESTS`, `UNIT_TESTS`, `S3_UNIT_TESTS`,
# `S3_TABLE_TESTS`, `OPENAPI_DRIFT_TESTS`, `TENANT_SCOPE_TESTS`,
# `QUERY_SCOPING_TESTS`, `SCHEMA_ISOLATION_TESTS`, `SCHEMA_ISOLATION_DB_TESTS`
# and the two-file split between them — are GONE with the tree they counted, and
# nothing carries them forward. Every one of them was either a count of
# `unittests src/lib.rs`, a count of `#[ignore]`d tests, or a count scoped to a
# named Rust test binary. There is no `src/lib.rs` to count, no `#[ignore]`, and
# `tests/` is deleted.
#
# Two of them described properties this tree does not have and this packet did not
# port, and that is recorded rather than silently dropped:
#
#   - `TENANT_SCOPE_TESTS` was `tests/tenant_scoping.rs` READING `src/store.rs`
#     AS TEXT and holding its `account_id` predicates in place. The Go tree holds
#     the same boundary a different way — `InAccountTx` is the only way to start a
#     transaction and it names the account — so there is no source file to read for
#     a dropped predicate, and a structural guard over a file that no longer
#     exists is a guard over nothing.
#   - `OPENAPI_DRIFT_TESTS` was `tests/openapi_document.rs`. The Go equivalent is
#     `internal/httpapi`'s drift tests against `openapi/v1.yaml`, which compare the
#     DOCUMENT to the router in both directions and are floored at 34 in
#     `bin/prime`.
#
# Neither is a loss the rewrite caused; both are the same properties expressed in
# the mechanism each tree has.
#
# THE TABLE ABOVE IS A COPY, AND IT WAS WRONG. Its httpapi row said 21 while
# `HTTPAPI_TESTS` below said 34, because three packets each raised the constant in
# the same commit that added a test — which is the rule this file exists to
# enforce — and none of them came back to the table. The same drift hit
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, which restates all four numbers for the same
# reason. Two hand-kept copies of a number nothing re-derives, both stale, and the
# constants they were describing were right the whole time.
#
# So the rows are named to the constants they mirror, because a reader who finds a
# disagreement now knows which side is the source: the constants. The gate reads
# the constants; nothing reads either table.

set -euo pipefail

# The floors. Read out of this tree, not carried forward from the Rust script.
VARIANT_TESTS=24
# The outbox publisher. NOT a database tier: its tests need no PostgreSQL (the
# loop is driven by a fake `Claimer`, the transport by an in-process JetStream
# broker of its own), so they live in the default tier and hold wherever the
# environment does.
OUTBOX_TESTS=19
PGTEST_DB_TESTS=17
# 49, and it was 39 with EIGHT TESTS of drift under it. The tenancy packet added
# `internal/service/tenancy_test.go`'s eight and did not raise this constant, so
# this script reported `expected 39, log says 47` on a run every test passed —
# which is the failure mode `bin/prime`'s FLOOR could not catch, because a floor
# is `-lt` and a count that is too HIGH passes one. Measured against this tree at
# 49 (the tenancy eight plus BRK-5's two payload guards).
SERVICE_DB_TESTS=52
# 34, and it MOVED THREE TIMES. 19, then the listing packet's two, then the
# idempotency packet's twelve, then the honest packet's one. Both halves of that are load-bearing and both are why the number
# is a claim rather than a constant:
#
#   - `go test -list` enumerates 34 without a database (the eleven `pgtest.New`
#     tests skip at runtime rather than disappearing), and the identity below
#     requires that count to equal this one. So a test that stopped running in the
#     `--db` tier is a failure in BOTH directions.
#   - `HTTPAPI_TESTS` is also the floor for `internal/httpapi` in `bin/prime`'s
#     `-list` step, where it has to hold on a bare machine.
HTTPAPI_TESTS=34

usage() {
  echo "usage: bin/tier-counts <log-of-'bin/prime --db'>" >&2
  exit 2
}

log="${1:-}"
[[ -n "$log" && -f "$log" ]] || usage

# `bin/prime` prints its step headers through printf with SGR escapes. That is not
# part of what is being measured and is not allowed to change an answer, so it is
# stripped before anything is counted.
clean="$(mktemp)"
trap 'rm -f "$clean"' EXIT
sed -e $'s/\x1b\\[[0-9;]*m//g' "$log" >"$clean"

fail() {
  printf '\033[1;31m==> tier-counts FAILED\033[0m  %s\n' "$1" >&2
  printf '    %s\n' "$2" >&2
  exit 1
}

# Count `--- PASS:` rows under one `==> ` step, attributed per PACKAGE.
#
# `go test -v` interleaves packages and prints the package name only on the `ok`
# line that comes AFTER that package's tests, so the rows are buffered and flushed
# on the next `ok`. Scanning forwards from a test row to find its package finds
# the NEXT package's tests instead, which reads a plausible number and is wrong.
#
# Only column-0 `--- PASS:` rows are counted: a subtest is indented four spaces
# (`    --- PASS: TestX/sub`), and counting those would make the total move when a
# table row gains a `t.Run`, which is a bookkeeping change rather than a claim
# somebody made.
#
# `[[:space:]]` AND NOT `[ \t]` for the `ok` line, and this is the same correction
# made in `gate.yml`'s countable proofs: `\t` inside a POSIX bracket expression
# is the two characters `\` and `t`, not a tab, in every awk that follows the
# standard. macOS's awk accepts it and GNU awk on a CI runner may not — so the
# class that reads correctly everywhere is used here instead. The symptom if this
# is ever reverted is every number below reading 0 on a green run.
#
# And a `-count=1` is required on the command this reads, or a cached run prints
# `ok <pkg> (cached)` with no `--- PASS:` rows at all and every number here reads
# zero. `bin/prime` carries the flag for that reason.
count_go() {
  local scope="${1:-}" package="${2:-}"
  awk -v scope="$scope" -v package="$package" '
    /^==>/ { group = $0; sub(/^==> /, "", group) }
    /^--- PASS: / { pending++ }
    /^ok[[:space:]]/ {
      if (scope == "" || group == scope) {
        split($0, fields, "\t")
        if (package == "" || fields[2] == package) total += pending
      }
      pending = 0
    }
    END { print total + 0 }
  ' "$clean"
}

# Read back what `bin/prime`'s `-list` step enumerated for one package.
#
# The line is `    ./internal/service: 39 tests`, printed by `assert_go_suite`.
# Reading it rather than recomputing it is the point of the identity below: this
# is the half of the comparison that ran WITHOUT a database, taken from the same
# log as the half that ran WITH one.
listed() {
  # The line is `    ./internal/service: 39 tests`, so the COUNT is field 2 and the
  # first field carries the trailing colon. Reading field 3 reads the word "tests"
  # and every package below would print the same thing.
  awk -v pkg="$1" '
    $1 == pkg":" { print $2; found = 1; exit }
    END { if (!found) print 0 }
  ' "$clean"
}

expect() {
  local what="$1" got="$2" want="$3"
  if [[ "$got" != "$want" ]]; then
    fail "$what" "expected $want, log says $got"
  fi
  printf '  ok  %-46s %s\n' "$what" "$got"
}

# --- the log is a `--db` run at all ------------------------------------------
# The database tiers exist only under the flag. A `bin/prime` log is missing their
# headers, and saying so is more useful than reporting a count of zero.
for step in \
  'go test -v ./internal/darkroomdb/... (against TEST_DATABASE_URL)' \
  'go test -v ./internal/service/... (against TEST_DATABASE_URL)'
do
  if ! grep -qxF "==> $step" "$clean"; then
    fail "tier ran" "'$step' is not in this log — this is not a complete 'bin/prime --db' run"
  fi
done

# The default-tier steps, because a log missing them is not this gate either.
for step in \
  'gofmt -l' \
  'go build ./...' \
  'go build the binary' \
  'go vet ./...' \
  'go test ./... (no database)' \
  'go test -list: the Go suites are not empty' \
  'go test -v ./internal/variants/ (no database)' \
  'go generate ./internal/api/ matches openapi/v1.yaml'
do
  if ! grep -qxF "==> $step" "$clean"; then
    fail "step ran" "'$step' is not in this log"
  fi
done

# --- the default tier --------------------------------------------------------
# The variant pipeline, pinned in the tier that runs it. A package that compiles,
# links, vets clean and answers nothing is the quietest failure in this
# repository, and twenty-four tests reporting zero is what it looks like from
# outside.
expect "default  variant-pipeline checks" \
  "$(count_go 'go test -v ./internal/variants/ (no database)' 'github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/variants')" \
  "$VARIANT_TESTS"

# The outbox publisher, pinned in the tier that runs it and counted for the reason
# the pipeline is: a package that compiles, links, vets clean and answers nothing
# is the quietest failure in this repository. This one is worse than usual,
# because the whole packet is a correctness claim ABOUT DELIVERY, and a drain loop
# that reported zero tests would look exactly like a drain loop with nothing to
# prove.
expect "default  outbox-publisher checks" \
  "$(count_go 'go test -v ./internal/outbox/ (no database)' 'github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/outbox')" \
  "$OUTBOX_TESTS"

# --- the database tiers ------------------------------------------------------
# Each of these three is a package whose tests are DATABASE tests, so on a machine
# with no PostgreSQL every one of them SKIPS and `go test ./...` prints `ok`. The
# floors in `bin/prime`'s `-list` step are what hold on a bare machine; these are
# what prove the tests RAN.
expect "database  harness and role checks" \
  "$(count_go 'go test -v ./internal/darkroomdb/... (against TEST_DATABASE_URL)' 'github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/darkroomdb/pgtest')" \
  "$PGTEST_DB_TESTS"
expect "database  service checks" \
  "$(count_go 'go test -v ./internal/service/... (against TEST_DATABASE_URL)' 'github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/service')" \
  "$SERVICE_DB_TESTS"
# `internal/httpapi` was counted here with no database tests in it, which was a
# FINDING packet 05 recorded rather than an accident: it was counted anyway, because
# a step whose count is not read is a step nobody is holding in place. The
# `Idempotency-Key` packet answered it — the ledger is only observable through the
# router and only against a real schema — so this is a database tier now, and the
# SKIP check below covers it.
expect "database  httpapi checks" \
  "$(count_go 'go test -v ./internal/httpapi/ (against TEST_DATABASE_URL; the Idempotency-Key ledger)' 'github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/httpapi')" \
  "$HTTPAPI_TESTS"

# A tier that reported a SKIP where a pass was expected is the failure this whole
# script is for, and `--- SKIP:` is the shape it takes: a package whose tests all
# skipped because no database answered, with a green `ok` underneath it.
for pair in \
  "go test -v ./internal/darkroomdb/... (against TEST_DATABASE_URL):github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/darkroomdb/pgtest" \
  "go test -v ./internal/service/... (against TEST_DATABASE_URL):github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/service" \
  "go test -v ./internal/httpapi/ (against TEST_DATABASE_URL; the Idempotency-Key ledger):github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/httpapi"
do
  step="${pair%%:*}"; pkg="${pair##*:}"
  skipped="$(awk -v scope="$step" '
    /^==>/ { group = $0; sub(/^==> /, "", group) }
    /^--- SKIP: / { if (group == scope) pending++ }
    /^ok[[:space:]]/ {
      if (group == scope) { split($0, f, "\t"); if (f[2] == pkg) total += pending }
      pending = 0
    }
    END { print total + 0 }
  ' "$clean")"
  if [[ "$skipped" != "0" ]]; then
    fail "the database tier skipped $skipped tests in $pkg" \
      "a --db run reported skips, so TEST_DATABASE_URL pointed somewhere this tier could not query: every one of them is a check that did not run"
  fi
done

# --- and the identity --------------------------------------------------------
# The one line here that is not a constant-to-constant comparison, and the one
# that would survive the constants being wrong together.
#
# ENUMERATED WITHOUT A DATABASE == PASSED WITH ONE, per package. The Rust script's
# `skipped == passed` is not translatable and the header says why; this is the
# version that is true of a Go tree, and it is checked in BOTH directions because
# each direction catches a different deletion:
#
#   enumerated > passed   a test stopped running in the database tier — a renamed
#                         helper, a build tag that stopped matching, a `t.Skip`
#                         that became unconditional.
#   enumerated < passed   the floor in `bin/prime` was raised past what the
#                         database tier runs, which means the two files disagree
#                         about the same package.
for pair in \
  "./internal/darkroomdb/pgtest:$PGTEST_DB_TESTS:github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/darkroomdb/pgtest" \
  "./internal/service:$SERVICE_DB_TESTS:github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/service" \
  "./internal/httpapi:$HTTPAPI_TESTS:github.com/cafaye/darkroom/internal/httpapi"
do
  pkg="${pair%%:*}"; rest="${pair#*:}"; want="${rest%%:*}"; import="${rest##*:}"
  got="$(listed "$pkg")"
  if [[ "$got" != "$want" ]]; then
    fail "enumerated == passed for $pkg" \
      "bin/prime enumerated $got without a database and tier-counts expects $want; those are the same tests and must be the same number — one of the two files is stale"
  fi
  printf '  ok  %-46s %s enumerated, %s passed with a database\n' "identity  $pkg" "$got" "$want"
done

printf '\n\033[1m==> tier-counts ok\033[0m  %d variants (no db), %d harness, %d service, %d httpapi (against the database)\n' \
  "$VARIANT_TESTS" "$PGTEST_DB_TESTS" "$SERVICE_DB_TESTS" "$HTTPAPI_TESTS"